    feat(admissions): open a trusted-side path for a first-ever admission
    
    `check_receipt_provenance` re-executes through `run_all.SKILL_CHECKS`
    imported from the validator's own tree, so a candidate can never declare
    the checks that grade it. That is the reason a candidate cannot vouch for
    itself and it is not weakened here. Its cost, unpaid until now, is that a
    *first-ever* admission is structurally impossible: the commit that adds a
    Skill is the commit that adds its row, and the gate reads that row from a
    branch the commit is not on yet. PR 68 (dynamic-workflow, Refs #59) died
    there on ADMISSION_STAMP_REFUSED:dynamic-workflow:NO_DECLARED_CHECKS, and
    every Skill already in the tree was admitted before ffe361a tightened the
    gate, so nothing had exercised this path since.
    
    `policy/bootstrap-admissions.json` is the one narrow way in, trusted on
    exactly the same terms: `admission_stamp.BOOTSTRAP` is REPO_ROOT-relative,
    never root-relative, so in CI it resolves inside .trusted while the graded
    tree is .candidate. An entry authorizes ONE Skill's first admission and
    pins the argv to execute plus the sha256 of the exact skill tree they may
    run against -- a candidate one byte away from the reviewed one is refused
    before anything of its executes, so an entry is an authorization for a
    reviewed tree rather than a standing key for a name.
    
    Nothing becomes a default in either direction. A Skill that owns a
    SKILL_CHECKS row is graded by it and never reads the allowlist, so
    re-admission stays refused when the trusted row disagrees; no entry falls
    through to the same NO_DECLARED_CHECKS refusal an unknown Skill has always
    had; and a malformed allowlist refuses loudly instead of decaying into the
    "no entry" shape.
    
    The entry is spent by the landing it authorizes rather than superseded by
    it: check_admissions reds with BOOTSTRAP_ENTRY_STALE on any entry whose
    Skill directory is present in the same tree, so the commit that lands the
    Skill must delete its own entry and no authorization outlives its
    admission. Both sides of that rule are read from one tree -- the
    candidate's -- because it can only add a refusal, and the only way to
    silence it is to perform the retirement being demanded.
    
    The dynamic-workflow entry ships here, authorizing PR 68's skill bytes at
    45126a80f7abb25c6261c4bf1ab716827f2afc8a1a7cd267fe7b4d15eb94d224. This
    branch is landing 1 of 2; PR 68 is landing 2 and carries the deletion.
    
    Refs ed3c/skill-concerns#72

